2026-08-25T00:00:07Z (RFD66606-2012) 'Context and Problem Statement
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2012  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:08Z (RFD66606-2012) 'The merge queue captures a PR's head commit the moment its checks pass and it is enqueued. While drafting the gitassembly tag MADR, a correction was pushed to the PR branch seconds after enqueueing; the queue had already snapshotted the earlier commit, so it merged the draft and orphaned the fix. The correction had to land as a second PR. How should a late fix to an in-flight PR be sequenced so it is not lost to the queue?
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2012  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:09Z (RFD66606-2012) 'Decision Drivers
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2012  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:10Z (RFD66606-2012) '- A pushed correction must be the commit that actually merges.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2012  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:11Z (RFD66606-2012) '- Avoid a follow-up PR that exists only to redo a missed fix.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2012  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:12Z (RFD66606-2012) '- Keep the cost of the rule near zero for the common case (no late fix).
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2012  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:13Z (RFD66606-2012) 'Considered Options
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2012  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:14Z (RFD66606-2012) '- Push the fix and assume the queue picks up the new tip (status quo that failed).
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2012  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:15Z (RFD66606-2012) '- Confirm the PR is still `OPEN` after the corrective push, before relying on it.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2012  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:16Z (RFD66606-2012) '- Dequeue the PR, push the fix, then re-enqueue.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2012  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:17Z (RFD66606-2012) 'Consequences
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2012  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:18Z (RFD66606-2012) '- Good: the commit that merges is the one intended, with no orphaned fixes.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2012  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:19Z (RFD66606-2012) '- Good: no redundant follow-up PRs to reapply a missed correction.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2012  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:20Z (RFD66606-2012) '- Bad: a small habit of checking state or dequeuing adds a step when editing a PR that is already in flight.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2012  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:21Z (RFD66606-2012) 'Confirmation
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2012  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:22Z (RFD66606-2012) 'A late fix to an enqueued PR is preceded by a dequeue or followed by an `OPEN` state check; the merged commit on `main` contains the fix, with no follow-up PR that only reapplies it.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2012  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:23Z (RFD66606-2012) 'More Information
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2012  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:24Z (RFD66606-2012) 'This refines the merge queue policy (`decisions/20260606-require-pr-and-merge-queue-on-main.md`) and is the work-in-progress discipline the bounded steering queue (`decisions/20260606-bounded-llm-steering-queue.md`) relies on: one finished concern per PR, merged before the next.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2012  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:25Z (RFD66606-2012) 'The problem
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
